    <p>So you got one of my business cards, awesome! Its also a tiny USB PD power supply, here's how to use it.</p>
    <p>Want to see everything its doing? Plug it into your computer and open the <a href="/bcv2/console/">console</a>.</p>
    <p><em>This page is a work in progress, so check back again soon!</em></p>

    <h2>What can it do?</h2>
    <h3>See what your charger offers</h3>
    <p>Plug it into a USB-C charger and the SOURCE CAPABILITY bar lights up every voltage it can give you, from 5 to 48V. The mode lights show which PD modes it supports (PDO, PPS, SAVS and EAVS). In a few seconds you know if that charger is any good.</p>
    <h3>Power your projects</h3>
    <p>Pick a voltage, press APPLY and the card negotiates it with the charger. Then press LOAD ON/OFF to send it to your load. A very slim and portable power supply, all you need is a PD charger and a cable!</p>

    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <h3>Plug it in</h3>
    <p>Plug a USB PD charger into the POWER port. The displays show the voltage, current and power going out, and the bar shows what the charger offers.</p>
    <h3>Hook up your load</h3>
    <p>Use the WIRE WEDGE (wedge the wires in, or clip a crocodile clip on it), banana plugs, or the 0.1" headers. Keep your load's capacitance under 90uF, its printed on the card too.</p>
    <h3>Change the voltage or current</h3>
    <p>Press V/A to choose voltage or current, then FINE + and FINE - for small steps or COARSE + and COARSE - for big ones. The new value blinks until you press APPLY.</p>
    <p>Dont want to press APPLY every time? hold it for AUTO APPLY, now every change goes through right away. Hold it again to turn it off.</p>
    <h3>Pick a mode</h3>
    <p>MODE picks between AUTO, PDO, PPS, SAVS and EAVS. Tap it once to see what the charger has, tap again to choose, then press APPLY (or just wait 5 seconds). If the new mode cant do your voltage, the card goes to the next one down, never higher.</p>
    <p>Hold MODE to turn EPR on or off, that's where 28V and up live.</p>
    <h3>Turn the output on</h3>
    <p>LOAD ON/OFF turns your output on and off, and LOAD EN lights up when its on. If LOAD EN blinks, the output cant turn on yet, or the change you're making will turn it off when you apply it.</p>
    <h3>Lock the buttons</h3>
    <p>Hold V/A for a second so nothing changes in your pocket, and hold it again to unlock. LOAD ON/OFF still works while its locked.</p>
    <h3>What the lights mean</h3>
    <p>THERM LIM means the card is dimming its display to keep its regulator happy, which happens at higher voltages. Nothing is wrong.</p>
    <p>If WARNING blinks, its spelling a letter in Morse code:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>R: the charger said no.</li>
      <li>S: your settings couldnt be saved.</li>
      <li>T: the power dropped out.</li>
      <li>F: the card lost power a few times while restoring your last setting, so it started with a safer one. Press APPLY to try again.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Chargers are all different</h2>
    <p>Not every charger offers every mode, and some just dont play nice with it. If something doesnt work or acts weird, tell you what, try another cable first - then another charger.</p>
    <h3>Only seeing 5V?</h3>
    <p>Then you arent using a full PD power supply, so thats all the card can get. Use a USB PD charger or powerbank thats atleast 20W, laptop chargers are usually the best ones.</p>
    <h3>Want 28V and up?</h3>
    <p>You'll need an EPR charger (these are usually 140W or more) and a 240W cable.</p>

    <h2>The console</h2>
    <h3>Connect</h3>
    <p>The card has a second USB port, DATA, and it only carries data. Keep your charger in POWER, plug DATA into your computer, then open <a href="/bcv2/console/">anasmalas.com/bcv2/console</a> in Chrome or Edge and press Connect. It works in Chrome on Android too.</p>
    <h3>What it does</h3>
    <p>You can see everything your charger offers in detail, request exact voltages, and watch live graphs of voltage, current and power. It also tells you if theres newer firmware for your card and how to update it.</p>
    <p>If your card restarts on its own, the console reads its black box when it reconnects. Thats a small log of what went wrong.</p>
    <h3>Please turn on bug reports</h3>
    <p>If you tick "Share anonymous bug reports", I get that black box and the log whenever something goes wrong, so I can look into your issue and fix it in the next firmware. It really helps!</p>
    <p>If its greyed out, I'm still setting it up. For now press "Report a bug", save the report, and send it to the email on the card. Scan the QR code on the card to add me to your phone contacts quickly :) Emailing it isnt anonymous of course, but the report still has your card's ID removed.</p>

    <h2 id="update">Updating the firmware</h2>
    <p>Two ways to do it. The easy flasher does it all for you, or you can grab the file and do it yourself.</p>
    <h3>Getting the card ready</h3>
    <p>Both ways need both USB ports. Plug DATA into your computer first, it only carries data so the card stays off. Then hold BOOT, plug your charger into POWER and let go. If the display comes on, it didnt make it into the bootloader. Unplug POWER and try again, pressing BOOT harder.</p>
    <h3>Easy flasher</h3>
    <p>It downloads the flashing tool and the latest firmware, then waits for your card and flashes it. The window goes green when its done and red if something went wrong. Got a few cards? Just keep plugging them in.</p>
    <p><strong>Windows:</strong> <a href="/bcv2/flash/bcv2-flasher.ps1" download>download bcv2-flasher.ps1</a>, right click it and pick "Run with PowerShell" (on Windows 11 it might be under "Show more options"). The first time, Windows needs a driver for the card. The flasher notices and walks you through Zadig, the usual tool for that.</p>
    <p><strong>Mac and Linux:</strong> open Terminal and run these:</p>
    <pre><code>cd ~/Downloads
curl -fLO https://anasmalas.com/bcv2/flash/bcv2-flasher.sh
bash bcv2-flasher.sh</code></pre>
    <p>On Linux it asks before adding a udev rule, so you dont need sudo every time.</p>
    <h3>Is it safe?</h3>
    <p>Tell you what, read it first! Both are short plain scripts and the <a href="https://github.com/AnasMalas/AnasMalas.github.io/tree/main/src/bcv2/flash" rel="noopener">source is on GitHub</a>. They only download <a href="https://github.com/ch32-rs/wchisp" rel="noopener">wchisp</a> v0.3.0, the open source flashing tool, from its GitHub page and the firmware from this site, and check both against fixed SHA-256 hashes. If anything doesnt match they delete it and stop.</p>
    <p>No admin needed, except for the Linux udev rule, and on Windows the Microsoft C++ runtime and the driver if you dont have them already. Those ask first. Everything else stays in one folder you can just delete: <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\bcv2-flasher</code> on Windows, <code>~/Library/Caches/bcv2-flasher</code> on Mac and <code>~/.cache/bcv2-flasher</code> on Linux.</p>
    <h3>Doing it yourself</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="/assets/bcv2/firmware/bcv2-rev1-product-latest.bin">Download the latest firmware</a>.</li>
      <li>Get a flashing tool: <a href="https://www.wch-ic.com/downloads/WCHISPTool_Setup_exe.html" rel="noopener">WCHISPTool</a> on Windows, or <a href="https://github.com/ch32-rs/wchisp" rel="noopener">wchisp</a> on Mac and Linux.</li>
      <li>Get the card ready like above.</li>
      <li>In WCHISPTool pick CH32X035, choose the file and press Download. With wchisp, run <code>wchisp flash</code> with the file.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Heads up, WCHISPTool and the easy flasher use different drivers on Windows, so stick to one.</p>
    <p>Either way, unplug POWER and plug it back in when its done. The console should now say you're on the latest version.</p>

    <h2 id="privacy">Bug reports and your data</h2>
    <h3>What gets sent</h3>
    <p>Nothing, unless you tick "Share anonymous bug reports" or send a report yourself. You can turn it off any time.</p>
    <p>A report has the firmware version, your browser and system (like Chrome 140 on Windows), the console log, the black box, and whatever you wrote.</p>
    <h3>What doesnt</h3>
    <p>Your card's ID is removed before anything is sent, and your IP address isnt stored. Please dont write personal info in there, I dont need it! The only other thing the console asks this site for is the latest firmware version.</p>

    <p>Have fun with it, and tag me when you make something cool :)</p>
